  "text": "We are given the equation:\n\n$$\n|z^2 - 9| = |z(z - 3i)|\n$$\n\nand asked to **find the smallest possible value of $|z - 2i|$**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Simplify the given equation\n\nWe start by expanding both sides.\n\n#### Left-hand side: $|z^2 - 9|$\n\nThis is the modulus of a complex number:  \n$$\n|z^2 - 9| = |z - 3i||z + 3i|\n$$\n\n#### Right-hand side: $|z(z - 3i)|$\n\nThis is the modulus of the product $z(z - 3i)$, which is:\n\n$$\n|z(z - 3i)| = |z| \\cdot |z - 3i|\n$$\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n$$\n|z - 3i||z + 3i| = |z||z - 3i|\n$$\n\nWe can cancel $|z - 3i|$ from both sides (assuming $z \\neq 3i$, which is valid for the modulus unless $z = 3i$, which would make the modulus undefined):\n\n$$\n|z + 3i| = |z|\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Solve the simplified equation\n\nWe are given:\n\n$$\n|z + 3i| = |z|\n$$\n\nThis is a geometric condition: the distance from $z$ to $-3i$ is equal to the distance from $z$ to the origin.\n\nThis describes the set of complex numbers $z$ that lie on the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining $0$ and $-3i$.\n\nSo:\n\n$$\nz \\in \\text{Perpendicular bisector of } \\{0, -3i\\}\n$$\n\nThis is a line in the complex plane. The perpendicular bisector of $0$ and $-3i$ is the imaginary axis.\n\nTherefore, the set of all such $z$ is:\n\n$$\nz = iy \\quad \\text{for some } i \\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nSo $z$ lies on the imaginary axis.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Find the smallest possible value of $|z - 2i|$\n\nLet $z = iy$, where $y \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThen:\n\n$$\n|z - 2i| = |iy - 2i| = |i(y - 2)| = |y - 2|\n$$\n\nSo we want to **minimize** $|y - 2|$, where $y \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe minimum value of $|y - 2|$ is **0**, which occurs when $y = 2$.\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $|z - 2i|$ is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{0}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{0}\n$$",
